  <p>
    <strong>MOT reminder software tracks each vehicle's MOT and service due date and sends a reminder ahead of it</strong>
    — by email or text — without a member of staff working through a spreadsheet of due dates every week. It's one
    part of what <a href="/garage-management-software">garage management software</a> covers, and matters most for <a href="/mot-test-centre-software">MOT test centres</a> running retests and repairs on the same board.
  </p>

  <h2>Why MOT reminders get missed without it</h2>
  <p>
    A workshop tracking MOT dates manually needs someone to check upcoming dates, message every customer, and log
    who has replied — every week, on top of the day job. It's the first thing to slip when the workshop is busy,
    which is exactly when a garage most needs the rebooking.
  </p>

  <h2>How automated reminders work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The due date is recorded once, against the vehicle, when it's first serviced or tested.</li>
    <li>Reminders go out on a schedule ahead of the due date, without manual triggering.</li>
    <li>Customers can rebook directly from the reminder, straight onto the <a href="/features/workshop-board">workshop board</a>, rather than having to call in.</li>
    <li>A missed reminder doesn't mean a missed booking — the system tracks it until it's actioned.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>MOT reminder rules for UK garages</h2>
  <p>
    UK MOT tests are due annually from a vehicle's third anniversary of registration (or first anniversary for
    taxis and some other vehicle classes), and DVSA sends its own reminder to the registered keeper. A garage's own
    reminder is a commercial one, separate from DVSA's — its job is to get the customer back into that specific
    workshop before they book elsewhere. See <a href="/pricing">pricing</a> for which plan includes it.
  </p>
